WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.180
Pac-Man, Gobble the Energizers or get gobbled by ghosts.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:28.060
And welcome to NEStalgia, chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.220
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.340
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:45.360
And right off the bat, we've got to discuss this chronologicalness because this is a weird time for chronological orders in NES games specifically released in North America because I'm not sure exactly where to put Pac-Man.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.320
It had an unlicensed version.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.620
It had a licensed version.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.720
Namco put out an official version in 1993.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.220
So, you know, I'm placing it here.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:59.940
But for all we know, we should have played Pac-Man months ago or we're playing it too early.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:07.000
Yeah. A few months ago, I played Pac-Man in preparation of one of these episodes.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.220
And I had learned that I had played the wrong game.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:17.000
So I went off after the unlicensed version, which I believe should be wrong regardless.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.960
Yeah. Unlicensed games are illegal for what it's worth.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.580
They are illegal.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.560
Well, you know, that brings up a good discussion.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.700
Are they illegal? Because Nintendo did fight this and pretty much won.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:50.080
Because Tenjin is the company that made this Pac-Man game really is just a subsidiary of Atari who wanted to make this spinoff label because they were they saw a loophole in one of Nintendo's rules that basically a developer could only release a certain amount of games for the NES in a particular year.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:51.540
And Atari didn't like that.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:52.800
So they created Tenjin.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:54.060
It's this way they could release more.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:01.840
And Tenjin also saw an opportunity to just reverse engineer the lockout chip on the NES.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:08.520
And so this way they could make chips without going through Nintendo's official seal and releasing them into the market.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.440
Nintendo didn't like that one bit.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:18.240
And they told Toys R Us and other game stores, like, if you sell Tenjin games, you won't be selling any official Nintendo games.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.260
So take your pick.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:22.060
That's pretty rough.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.480
Very the man of them.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.800
Right. We can talk about, like, whether that was cool, you know, or legal.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:36.520
But we're not going to do that because we're just going to focus on Pac-Man the game here.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:43.400
And before we even talk about the NES version, I just want to hear your guys, like, familiarity with Pac-Man.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.240
Like, when do you first remember playing Pac-Man?

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:46.700
What does Pac-Man mean to you?

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.000
He is kind of a video game icon.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.420
So, Joe, let's start with you.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.220
I mean, it's hard to really remember, like, my first...

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.600
Obviously, there's so many versions of Pac-Man.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.960
You can play Pac-Man on so many different, like, platforms.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.860
The one that I remember the most is not by any means the classic Pac-Man, but there was...

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:14.880
It was on, like, my Windows computer and it was just some random program that was, like, an endless Pac-Man.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:21.540
It was, like, each level was, like, five or six of these one Pac-Man screen screens.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:27.980
So, when you go off to the little thing to the left or the right that usually loops you around, it would take you to another screen.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.580
And it had, like, a jazzy music to it.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.360
I don't think it was a very popular thing.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:39.980
I just remember, like, playing that on, like, on our old, like, Windows 98 computer for a while.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.900
But then, obviously, I've played a lot of other things.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.220
Like, a couple years ago, somebody had, like, this...

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.580
I think it's, like, a moderately popular, like, Pac-Man game, but he had it on his Roku TV.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.820
And I don't remember what it's called, but it was just, like, another, like, endless Pac-Man thing.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:56.760
Yeah, but, you know...

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.740
Pac-Man 256, I think it was.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:03:59.960
Yes, that's the one. Yeah, that's it.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:05.020
I knew that that one was kind of recognizable, so I was hoping one of you guys would remember what it was.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.720
Yeah, so... I remember the first time I played Super Mario.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.500
And I remember the first time I played a lot of games from this era.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:22.100
But, like, because nobody in our age bracket can seem to remember the first time they played Pac-Man,

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:26.640
I can only imagine that it's just you're born with the knowledge.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.460
Because, yeah, I have no idea the first time I played this was probably when I was three or four years old.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.400
But, no, I mean, I enjoy it.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:44.180
I can't get past, like, the third level, but it's got a very satisfying quality to it.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.560
You know, I actually can remember...

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:55.160
I wouldn't say, like, remember the date, but I can remember playing this on my grandmother's NES one day when we went over there.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.940
They had Super Mario Bros., Pac-Man, and I want to say Paperboy.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:09.560
And I remember playing Pac-Man, and I think what's cool about Pac-Man is that from the time I first played it until now, I haven't gotten any better.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.780
I just... I play Pac-Man.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.340
To Sean's point, I enjoy Pac-Man.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:21.720
Pac-Man, it satisfies, and I think that that's, like, that's the universal appeal of a game like Pac-Man,

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:28.860
is that there are certain people who know, like, well, if you go in this exact order, if you go in this route for this map,

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:32.160
the ghost will never touch you, and you'll be able to eat all four of them all the time.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.240
And it's like, that's one way to play a game.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:40.080
But then there's also just, like, a satisfactory way of playing something that's just for fun,

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:45.420
that, like, people who never play video games will still play something like Pac-Man, Pong.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:51.600
I don't know what else really fits into that category of, like, so simple that anyone can enjoy them.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.240
But Pac-Man definitely...

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:54.640
Yeah, Centipede. There you go.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.620
Again, Space Invader's probably more likely than Centipede.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:05.260
Yeah, I think Pac-Man just had something about him, you know, so early on.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:08.560
You know, there aren't other games like that.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:13.240
Like, why have we not seen other games that just take place in, like, an enclosed maze,

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:16.900
and you just have to, like, traverse through it, you know?

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.900
Like, we don't... that doesn't exist as a genre.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:22.720
I feel like because it's so simple that any way that you do it,

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:26.100
if you're going to be close enough to Pac-Man, will just look like a ripoff of Pac-Man.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.540
You know, it's, like, such a simple gameplay style.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.400
So you go through a maze and you collect the dots.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:38.320
Then there's just something very satisfying about just systematically cleaning up this board.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.580
Like, I think that's really what it is.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:46.480
Just seeing your progress, like, very visibly on the screen is, like, what, to me, is, like, that's so satisfying.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:49.480
You're just kind of, like, vacuuming up all these...

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:53.840
I'm making it sound really boring, but you're just, like, cleaning up this whole board, and by the end...

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:55.300
No, you're right. I did that.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.060
I undid everything that was on this board.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:01.360
Yeah, that... When you have that feeling of...

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:04.600
Yeah, I guess I can't really describe it in any other words,

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:07.820
but if you are...

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:12.040
If you have a bunch of stuff on the board and your goal is to get rid of it,

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.720
that is, in itself, a satisfying...

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:18.460
I don't know if it's, like, something deep in our psyche, but...

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:23.360
Yeah, dude, I mean, I get satisfied when I mow the lawn and I look back and I'm like,

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.060
wow, it's like I'm a short of the grasses.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:28.980
I did all that. Like, that's what this is, like, scratching that same itch.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:35.760
For sure. And sure, Pac-Man could get a game where he mows the lawn and that happens, you know,

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:38.960
but it is interesting that nobody decided to also make a game like that.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:44.300
And to be clear, there are plenty of Pac-Man clones on all sorts of early consoles.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:47.540
I think, like, the Atari 2600, the ColecoVision, the Intellivision,

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:53.400
they all had Pac-Man clones, but nothing was able to take over

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.400
what made Pac-Man such a strong game and stop its domination,

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.940
and people were really just waiting for Pac-Man to come to those consoles appropriately.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:08.620
So if you had beaten Pac-Man to the Atari 2600 and created basically the same game,

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.540
it still didn't win because it wasn't Pac-Man.

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:14.120
I think that says something about a game like this, right?

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:15.880
I think so.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:20.880
So, you know, we talked about that Pac-Man's enjoyable and that it satisfies,

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:26.000
but other than just clearing the stage, what makes the game fun to you guys?

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:31.080
You know, the ghosts that are chasing you, Blinky, the red one, Pinky, the pink one,

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.900
Inky, who I believe is referred to as Cyan, and Clyde, who's orange.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:40.220
You know, these four ghosts that just traverse the screen,

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.280
they don't really have any rhyme or reason either.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.760
I wouldn't say they're, like, well-programmed AI, right?

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.820
I think it's somewhere built into the code that one of them follows you,

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.640
another one roams, another one purposefully, like, dodges corners that you don't dodge.

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:57.320
I know there is some design logic there, but for the most part,

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.840
you're just kind of like, it's this game of tag, right?

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:10.700
Yeah, I mean, I wouldn't dismiss the creative AI that they put into the game outright,

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:13.440
because I think it is very creative the way that they made it,

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:16.480
so that it's not just, like, you get a conga line behind you,

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:19.640
because this game very easily could have fallen into that.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:24.880
So that it's always just a matter of time until you're cornered.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:31.180
But, yeah, I think that without that, this game would have been way too easy,

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:34.780
and it would not have the same legacy that it does now.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:37.840
Yeah, I was going to mention, too, I actually had forgotten

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:41.460
until a little bit into playing this game that I had heard, you know,

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.400
in my adult life of some time that the ghost had such specific programming,

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:50.800
and I think that's really cool, and I'm still not familiar enough with them

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:53.740
to be able to, like, use that to my advantage or use that information,

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.120
but that makes me a little more interested in, like, playing more just to see,

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:02.440
like, if you watch someone who's, like, good at this game play it,

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:05.420
it looks like they do, they are aware, okay, like, Pinky's coming at me,

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:08.780
but I know the way Pinky moves, so I know kind of how to counter that right off the bat.

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:12.460
I think that's a cool thing, and it's also just a fun bit of knowledge

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:15.000
where I feel like not everybody knows that when they play Pac-Man,

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:18.900
so it's kind of a cool little Easter egg to drop on someone.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:22.700
Yeah, it's a great topic for parties.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:27.120
Yeah. And there's not much else going on, right?

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:30.400
Like, you have the power pellets, and you have the fruit

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.620
that appear in the center of the screen,

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:37.820
but it's not like the game gets more complicated as you go on.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:40.960
If I understand correctly, it's just that the ghosts get faster,

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:44.920
and the time that you have between the power pellet and the ghosts...

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:46.460
Yeah. Like, everything gets faster.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:47.700
Everything just gets faster, right.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.800
So, what do you think about that as an approach?

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:54.420
Like, we always complain about, you know, games that don't have enough going on

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.080
after a certain amount of gameplay loop.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.520
Is this simple enough that it's just fine,

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:00.980
and it's almost just, like, your brain...

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:02.840
You know, it's using, like, a separate part of your brain

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:04.260
that just enjoys doing this?

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:11.880
Yes. I personally would have liked to see, like, vastly changed layouts of the maze,

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:15.300
because I think another thing that makes this game fun

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:19.740
is that it's not just, oh my god, I gotta get all the power pellets

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:21.720
or whatever they're called, energy pellets or whatever,

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:23.280
and not get hit by the ghosts,

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:27.540
but it's also, like, effectively managing your route

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:32.400
to kind of, like, take as little time as possible,

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:34.380
at least for me, so you're in danger.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:36.920
You know, like, I don't want to miss a bunch of dots

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:39.100
in the bottom left corner and have to come back to it later.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:42.200
Like, I want to be able to make every, like, pass...

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:43.100
Yeah, you want to be efficient.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:44.160
...every passage, like, capitalized.

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:48.680
Yeah, exactly. And I imagine that if you're playing this a lot

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:49.620
and you're getting really good at it

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.640
and it's always the same layout of the board,

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:54.420
even if it gets faster, you kind of will just have, like,

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.020
go-to strategies for every level,

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:58.580
and it'll stop challenging you on that level,

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:01.140
and then it'll just become a test of your reflexes,

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.580
and it might just lose that more cerebral aspect of it

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:07.520
that it would have if it was, like, a new layout every time.

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:10.140
You also have to remember that

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:12.920
not only are you getting this moment-to-moment gameplay,

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.160
and it's, you know, it's very tight in that regard,

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:19.280
but every couple levels you do get a slice

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:23.740
of the very involved story of Pac-Man.

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:26.260
Right, that's true.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:27.780
The cutscenes are...

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:30.680
Some people refer to them as the first video game cutscenes.

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:33.520
I think they're right.

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:37.260
Yeah, I think it's kind of funny to call them cutscenes at all,

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:40.960
but it is nice that you get anything

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:43.460
other than just the next maze, you know?

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:47.000
Because technically, Joe, as we talked about earlier,

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:48.940
you know, the Pac-Man 256 thing,

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:52.440
that's alluding to the fact that there are 256 mazes,

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:54.640
and then you get to the kill screen,

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:58.120
which basically is just, like, a nonsense screen

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:00.940
that glitches out on you and forces the game to end.

00:13:01.340 --> 00:13:03.920
You know, that's pretty much all Pac-Man has to offer,

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:07.700
is just 256 mazes as it gets faster and faster.

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:09.960
I'm sure there is a limit so that, like, after...

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:11.840
Who knows? That's legible.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:14.100
Maybe even the first 20 stages,

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:18.100
it's like the speed just remains constant from that point on.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:21.220
I am not a pro Pac-Man player and don't know.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:23.800
But I think it's kind of interesting

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:27.720
that future Pac-Man games would try

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:30.580
to, like, add on to the Pac-Man formula,

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:31.640
and you guys have...

00:13:31.640 --> 00:13:33.880
I guarantee you've never heard of these games.

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:35.560
Like, after Pac-...

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:36.480
Well, Mike, Mike, Mike,

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:38.900
before we go into, like, the...

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.700
Oh, I'm not doing sequels and spinoffs.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:44.160
I'm bringing up a point here

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.280
about the simplicity of the game,

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:49.000
you know, and how it works in its favor,

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.660
because a lot of times we pick on simple games as, like,

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:54.600
yeah, I mean, all you could really do in this game

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.640
was just shoot, so that wasn't that fun.

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:57.820
But meanwhile, it's like, yeah,

00:13:57.820 --> 00:13:59.580
all you can do is eat dots in this game,

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:02.060
but we're loving it. Yeah, but that's the difference

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:03.700
between simplicity and elegance,

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:06.220
and I think that Pac-Man is an elegant game.

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:08.280
I want to talk about, you know,

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:09.620
and this is not sequels and spinoffs,

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:11.660
but I want to talk about Pac-Man Plus,

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:14.920
Super Pac-Man, Baby Pac-Man,

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.420
Professor Pac-Man, and Pac and Pal.

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:19.620
These are the first arcade games

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:21.020
in a line of, like, who knows?

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:23.040
There's countless Pac-Man games.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:24.540
So these are the first

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:26.320
in a series of Pac-Man arcade games

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:28.640
that I guarantee you guys have no idea

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:30.320
what any of those are

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:31.660
and what makes them different

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:33.120
than regular Pac-Man.

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:35.060
Am I right? I haven't played any of those.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:36.740
Joe? Okay, yeah.

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.040
Yeah, because it's like,

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:41.900
it's interesting. So Pac-Man Plus is virtually

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:43.720
the same exact game

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:45.200
as regular Pac-Man,

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:47.180
except for that

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:49.540
